<!-- =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D TIPS =3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D -->
<section id=3D"tips">
  <div class=3D"section-label">05 =C2=B7 Exam Technique</div>
  <h2 class=3D"section-title">Pro Tips for<br>IGCSE Exams</h2>
  <p class=3D"section-desc">These tips will help you score full marks on tr=
ace table questions in your IGCSE Computer Science exam.</p>

  <div class=3D"tips-list fade-in visible">
    <div class=3D"tip-item open">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=E2=9C=8F=EF=B8=8F</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Only write when a variable changes</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        In a trace table, you should only enter a value in a cell if that v=
ariable's value <strong>changes</strong> on that line. If the variable keep=
s the same value, leave the cell blank (or draw a dash). Writing the same v=
alue again in every row is a common mistake that can lose marks.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=94=81</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Always check the loop condition BEFORE en=
tering the loop body</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        For WHILE loops, you must evaluate the condition at the START of ev=
ery iteration, including the very first one. If the condition is already FA=
LSE before the loop begins, the loop body never executes. This is a key dif=
ference between WHILE and REPEAT-UNTIL loops.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=94=A2</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Know your FOR loop boundaries</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        A <code>FOR i =E2=86=90 1 TO 5</code> loop runs with i =3D 1, 2, 3,=
 4, 5 =E2=80=94 that is <strong>5 iterations</strong>. The loop counter goe=
s UP TO and INCLUDING the final value. Don't accidentally stop at 4. Also n=
ote: <code>FOR i =E2=86=90 1 TO 5 STEP 2</code> gives i =3D 1, 3, 5.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=93=8A</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Add ALL variable columns =E2=80=94 even t=
emporary ones</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        If a question asks you to <em>create</em> the trace table, include =
a column for every variable that appears in the code, including loop counte=
rs (like i or n) and temporary swap variables. Missing a column means you c=
an't show your working, which costs marks.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=8E=AF</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Watch for off-by-one errors</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        A very common bug (and exam trap) is looping one too many or too fe=
w times. Double-check your final condition: does <code>count &lt; 5</code> =
stop at count=3D4 (4 iterations) or count=3D5 (5 iterations)? The answer: i=
t stops WHEN count reaches 5, so the last loop body runs with count=3D4.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=94=84</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Swap variables need a TEMP variable</span=
>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
      </button>
      <div class=3D"tip-content">
        When tracing a swap operation, you'll typically see: <code>temp =E2=
=86=90 a</code>, <code>a =E2=86=90 b</code>, <code>b =E2=86=90 temp</code>.=
 Make sure your trace table includes the <strong>temp</strong> column. If y=
ou skip it, you'll incorrectly show both a and b getting the same value.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"tip-item">
      <button class=3D"tip-toggle">
        <span class=3D"tip-icon">=F0=9F=A7=A0</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-title">Finding errors: compare expected vs actua=
l</span>
        <span class=3D"tip-chevron">=E2=96=BC</span>
